- Give the body only what it needs. Food, drink, clothing, house, and household only as far as need requires; cut off everything for show or luxury (Handbook 33).

- Let the body be the measure. The body measures what you should own as the foot measures the shoe. Go past the measure and there is no stopping; the shoe becomes gilded, then purple, then jeweled (Handbook 39).

- Tend the body in passing. Spending much time on exercise, eating, drinking, and the body's other functions is a sign of a dull mind. Give your main strength to reason (Handbook 41).

- Be chaste, without judging others. Keep yourself pure before marriage, but do not be harsh with those who are not, and do not boast of your own conduct (Handbook 33).

- Train in secret. If you live frugally, do not boast of it. If you train yourself in hardship, do it for yourself and not for show: when you are very thirsty, rinse your mouth with water and tell no one (Handbook 47).

- Take the smaller share. At a shared meal, the largest portion suits the appetite but not the fellowship (Handbook 36).

- Bear illness as a test. Fever, thirst, and hunger are the time to show what your training was for. Bear them without blaming God or man (Discourses III.10).$q$, 218, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$corpus_verified$q$]::text[], true),
