- Feed the habits you want and starve the rest. Every habit grows by the acts that match it. To end one, stop doing it and do something else in its place. Count the days you were not angry: every day, then every other day, then every third (Discourses II.18).

- Train hardest against your own weakness. If you lean toward pleasure, lean past the middle the other way for training. If you are irritable, practice being insulted without being vexed (Discourses III.12).

- Begin with small things. When a little oil is spilled or a little wine stolen, say, "This is the price of peace of mind" (Handbook 12).

- Be able to be alone. Be sufficient for yourself and your own companion, with thoughts to occupy you (Discourses III.13).

- Treat your rules as laws. Keep the rules you adopt as if breaking them would be sacrilege, and ignore what anyone says about it (Handbook 50).

- Stop postponing. You are no longer a boy. Now is the contest and it cannot be put off. You are not yet Socrates, but live as one who wants to be (Handbook 50).

- Practice first, proof second, logic last. The first thing is to not lie; the second, knowing why; the third, knowing why the proof holds. We do it backwards, and lie while proving that lying is wrong (Handbook 51).

- Know the signs of progress. The one making progress blames no one, praises no one, and accuses no one. He looks to himself for all help and harm, smiles at praise, makes no defense against criticism, and watches himself like an enemy lying in ambush (Handbook 48).$q$, 271, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$corpus_verified$q$]::text[], true),
