- Treat the body strictly so it obeys the mind. Indulge it only as far as health requires. Eat to end hunger, drink to end thirst, dress to keep out the cold, and live in a house that keeps out the weather; thatch shelters as well as marble (Letters 8).

- But do not torture it for show. Living by nature does not mean being dirty on purpose, dressing in rags, or eating disgusting food to be admired. Seeking out dainties is luxury; avoiding ordinary, inexpensive things is madness (Letters 5).

- Measure wealth by desire, not by holdings. The poor man is not the one who has little but the one who craves more. The right limit is first what is necessary, then what is enough (Letters 2).

- Live by nature, not opinion. "If you live according to nature, you will never be poor; if according to opinion, you will never be rich." Nature needs little; opinion is never satisfied (Letters 16, quoting Epicurus).

- Rehearse poverty. Set aside days to live on the cheapest food and roughest clothes, and ask yourself, "Is this what I feared?" Toughen the soul while fortune is kind (Letters 18).

- Keep exercise short. Choose simple exercises that tire the body quickly and save your time for the mind (Letters 15).

- Never get drunk. Drunkenness is madness taken on purpose; stretch it over several days and no one would doubt the man was insane (Letters 83).$q$, 238, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$corpus_verified$q$]::text[], true),
