- Do not suffer before the event. More things frighten us than crush us; we suffer more in imagination than in reality. Do not be unhappy before the crisis comes (Letters 13).

- Change your soul, not your scenery. Travel does not cure a troubled mind, because you take yourself along. As Socrates said, you travel with yourself (Letters 28).

- Find quiet inside the noise. You are at peace when no noise reaches you and no word, flattering or threatening, shakes you out of yourself. Still, when you can avoid the uproar, avoid it (Letters 56).

- Welcome hardship as training. God does not pet the good man; he tests him, hardens him, and fits him for himself. The veteran looks calmly at his own wounds; the recruit turns pale at the thought of one (On Providence 1, 4).

- Set your own limit on success. Nothing frees you from hope and fear like halting of your own accord, before fortune chooses to stop you (On Peace of Mind 10).$q$, 166, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$corpus_verified$q$]::text[], true),
