- Delay is the greatest cure for anger. Ask anger for time, not to pardon the offense but to judge it rightly. If it waits, it ends (On Anger II.29).

- Ask whether you have done the same. Before condemning, ask whether you have ever committed that fault or made that mistake yourself (On Anger II.28 to 29).

- Review every day before you sleep. Ask what bad habit you cured today, what fault you resisted, and in what way you are better. Anger calms when it knows it must face a judge each night (On Anger III.36).

- Treat those below you as you would be treated by those above. Remember that someone has as much power over you as you have over those who serve you (Letters 47).

- Give and forget; receive and remember. The giver should forget at once that he gave; the receiver should never forget that he received. Never remind anyone of what you did for them (On Benefits II.10).

- Serve however you still can. If public life is closed to you, serve in private. If you cannot speak, help with silent counsel. Socrates kept encouraging Athens under the Thirty (On Peace of Mind 4 to 5).$q$, 198, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$corpus_verified$q$]::text[], true),
