- Avoid the crowd. Seneca came home from the crowds and the games more greedy, more ambitious, more self-indulgent, and even more cruel. Nothing damages character so much as lounging at the shows (Letters 7).

- Judge a friend before, trust him after. Think long about whether to admit someone to your friendship. Once you have, trust him as you trust yourself (Letters 3).

- Choose a guardian and live under his eyes. Pick a Cato, or if Cato is too severe, a gentler Laelius, and picture him always as your protector and pattern. You cannot straighten what is crooked without a ruler (Letters 11).

- Read few authors, deeply. Linger with a limited number of master thinkers and digest them. Everywhere means nowhere; the one who travels constantly has many acquaintances and no friends (Letters 2).

- Be able to stay put. The first sign of a well-ordered mind is the ability to remain in one place and keep your own company (Letters 2).$q$, 160, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$corpus_verified$q$]::text[], true),
