- He saved Alcibiades at Potidaea. Alcibiades says Socrates rescued him when he was wounded, and then insisted the prize for valor go to Alcibiades instead of himself (Symposium 220d to 220e).

- He kept his head in the retreat from Delium. While the army fled, Socrates walked calmly with Laches, looking so formidable that no one dared attack them (Symposium 221a to 221b; Laches 181b).

- He drank the hemlock calmly. He comforted his weeping friends, took the cup without trembling, and his last words asked Crito to pay a debt of a rooster to Asclepius (Phaedo 116b to 118a).$q$, 98, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$unverified$q$]::text[], true),
