- He walked barefoot on the ice at Potidaea. In a bitter winter, when other soldiers wrapped their feet in felt, he went out in his ordinary cloak and marched barefoot (Symposium 220a to 220b).

- He could drink all night and never get drunk. At Agathon's banquet he outlasted everyone, then left at dawn to spend the day as usual (Symposium 220a, 223c to 223d).

- He resisted Alcibiades. The most desired young man in Athens tried to seduce him, and Socrates declined, valuing Alcibiades's soul over his beauty (Symposium 217a to 219d).$q$, 91, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$unverified$q$]::text[], true),
