- He refused to escape from prison. When Crito arranged his escape, Socrates refused, arguing that one must never return wrong for wrong, and letting the Laws of Athens make their case to him (Crito 49a to 54d).

- He preferred an unjust death to a just one. When a friend lamented that he was being put to death unjustly, Socrates asked whether he would rather see him put to death justly (Xenophon, Apology 28).$q$, 73, $q$original$q$, $q$https://github.com/Kylejemery/arete-app/blob/main/academy/corpus-ingestion/synthesis/virtues-of-socrates.v1.md$q$, 2026, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$synthesis$q$, $q$english$q$, $q$Intermediate$q$, array[$q$unverified$q$]::text[], true),
